The default setting of the --on-error should be:

- from the start of processing options (--init, --eval, --load)
  including loading the init file (rc file), the default --on-error
  setting is exit

- for interactive REPL (the --repl or default interactife mode), the
  default --on-error is debug.


Otherwise the --on-error sets the behavior for all the following
options and working modes.

If no --init or --no-init option is given, then the init file is loaded
with --on-error exit.

The current setting of --on-error is kept for the following options until the next --on-error:

| command:                     | on error:                             |
|------------------------------+---------------------------------------|
| bocl \                       |                                       |
| --on-error debug \           |                                       |
| --init ~/foo.lisp \          | debug loading foo.lisp                |
| --on-error appease \         |                                       |
| --load ~/lib.lisp \          | appease loading lib.lisp              |
| --on-error exit \            |                                       |
| --eval '(print (lib:foo))' \ | print and exit if expression errors   |
| --on-error abort \           |                                       |
| --eval '(/ 0)' \             | print divide-by-zero and continue     |
| --repl                       | the REPL is executed with the last    |
|                              | setting of --on-error, ie abort here. |

The user may add --on-error debug before --repl to get the debugger.

| command: | on error:                                             |
|----------+-------------------------------------------------------|
| bocl     | ~/.bocl is loaded with --on-error exit by default. |
|          | The REPL is entered with --on-error debug by default. |


| command:         | on error:                                  |
|------------------+--------------------------------------------|
| bocl \           |                                            |
| --on-error debug | ~/.bocl is loaded with --on-error debug    |
|                  | The REPL is entered with --on-error debug  |

| command:             | on error:       |
|----------------------+-----------------|
| bocl \               |                 |
| --init ~/foo.lsp \   | exit  (default) |
| --load ~/lib.lisp \  | exit  (default) |
| --eval '(lib:foo)' \ | exit  (default) |
| --repl               | debug (default) |


----------------------------------------------------------------------
PROGRESS (slice 118, v1.3.5)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
DONE — the default split:
- --on-error is no longer pre-set to "exit": on_error_action defaults to NULL
  (sources/main.c).  When --on-error is NOT given, option/rc/file processing runs
  under EXIT (the C on_error default), and the interactive REPL is published
  BOCL:*ON-ERROR* = :DEBUG.  When --on-error IS given, that action is used for both
  the processing and the REPL.  (on_error_explicit tracks this.)
- This satisfies the common cases:
    bocl                         -> rc/files EXIT, REPL DEBUG
    bocl --on-error debug        -> everything DEBUG
    bocl --init f --load g --repl-> rc/files EXIT, REPL DEBUG
- Side fix: the spurious "WARNING: argument for --on-error has already been given:
  exit ; changing it to debug" (from the original debugger issue) is gone, since the
  default is no longer a pre-set "exit" string.
- Documented in man/bocl.1, documentation/documentation.org, documentation/bocl.texi.

REMAINING (kept open — larger refactor):
- Per-option, command-line-ORDER interleaving (the first table in this issue):
  --on-error currently applies globally to all pre-REPL processing and to the REPL,
  not per following option.  Honouring "the current --on-error is kept for the
  following options until the next --on-error" needs the launcher to build ONE
  ordered action list (interleaving --on-error with -i/-l/-x) instead of the present
  separate initfiles / expressions_and_loads lists processed in phases.
- Pre-REPL DEBUG: an error while loading -i/-l (or the rc) under --on-error debug
  does not yet enter the Lisp debugger — the pre-REPL path uses the C error handler
  (kernel ERROR), whose invoke_debugger is a stub.  Routing pre-REPL form evaluation
  through the same *ON-ERROR* / %REPL-EVAL mechanism as the REPL would make debug/
  abort/appease/exit uniform across processing and the REPL.


There is an inconsistency in the specifications between -i and -l and
--on-error ordered semantics.

So to clarify, if there's an --on-error before the first
-norc|--no-init or -i|--init, then it will apply on loading the RC
file and all the init files, otherwise the default --on-error exit
applies.  The init files are collected first, and the RC file (if
-norc|--no-init is not given) and the init files are loaded.

Note these files may change the setting of BOCL:*ON-ERROR* and this
applies immediately, and for the following init files and -l|-x
arguments, unless a new --on-error is given for the -l|-x arguments.

So basically, the `--on-error …` arguments after the first -i|--init
are equivalent to `-x '(setf bocl:*on-error* …)'` and are processed in
order with -x and -l options.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
PROGRESS (v1.4.9) — REMAINING items DONE; issue closed
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Both items from the previous REMAINING section are now implemented:

1. Command-line-ORDER interleaving of --on-error with -i/-l/-x.
   sources/main.c now builds, in addition to the phase-1 `initfiles` list (-i,
   loaded after the rc file), ONE ordered phase-2 action list of -x (eval), -l
   (load) and the --on-error settings that appear AFTER the first -i.  The
   actions run in command-line order, an `act_seterr` mutating BOCL:*ON-ERROR*
   immediately — exactly like -x '(setf bocl:*on-error* ACTION)'.  An --on-error
   BEFORE the first -i (or --no-init) sets the init-phase policy (last wins, else
   EXIT) used for the rc + init files and carried into phase 2; one AFTER it is
   an ordered action.  The REPL uses the last value when any --on-error was
   given, else DEBUG.  (This is the -i-centric resolution stated at the bottom of
   this issue; -x/-l before an --on-error with no -i present therefore collapse
   to the init-phase value, as specified.)

2. Pre-REPL evaluation routed through the SAME *ON-ERROR* / %REPL-EVAL machinery
   as the REPL.  New bocl_toplevel_eval()/bocl_toplevel_eval_cstring() in bocl.c
   call the Lisp %REPL-EVAL wrapper (falling back to a direct eval on the bare
   kernel).  run_session loads rc/init files as (load "...") forms and runs all
   -x/-l actions through it, so :debug enters the Lisp debugger, :appease/:abort
   report and continue, and :exit quits with cleanups — uniformly across
   processing and the REPL.  apply_on_error() keeps the C `on_error` enum (and
   thus bocl_load_recover for per-form appease recovery) in sync with
   BOCL:*ON-ERROR*.  The dead C error_handler()/invoke_debugger() stubs are left
   as-is (never reached).

Verified on the built binary: default EXIT (pre-REPL error -> exit 1);
--on-error abort/appease continue; ordered `--on-error abort -i foo --on-error
exit -x '(error)'` honours each setting in turn; init-phase EXIT vs explicit
policy for -i files; appease per-form load recovery; and --on-error debug
pre-REPL now ENTERS the Lisp debugger (backtrace + restart menu) instead of a C
exit.  No regression in the CUnit suites that run here.  man/bocl.1,
documentation.org/.texi/.info and the pdf updated to describe the ordering.
